ICML-2024-P1665,ICML-2024-A008309,William Powell,shared,3,2,2,"where Θ ⊆ Rp is a convex, but not necessarily compact feasible set and θ represents the parameters of a model to be optimized. Here V is a finite index set where one can view each index v ∈ V representing (a batch of) data that can be accessed at once. Then f v (θ) is the loss incurred using parameter θ with respect to data at v, which is weighted by π(v) ≥ when forming the overall objective f in ( ). Without loss of generality, we assume the π(v)s sum to one. When π(v) ≡ | V | the problem ( ) becomes the classical finite sum problem in the optimization literature. Instances of non-uniform π arise when training a model with imbalanced data as has been studied in (Steininger et al., ; Wang et al., 2022b; Sow et al., ).",-2,data/raw/icml/2024/first_pages/ICML-2024-P1665.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v235/main/assets/powell24a/powell24a.pdf
ICML-2024-P1665,ICML-2024-A008310,Hanbaek Lyu,shared,3,2,2,"where Θ ⊆ Rp is a convex, but not necessarily compact feasible set and θ represents the parameters of a model to be optimized. Here V is a finite index set where one can view each index v ∈ V representing (a batch of) data that can be accessed at once. Then f v (θ) is the loss incurred using parameter θ with respect to data at v, which is weighted by π(v) ≥ when forming the overall objective f in ( ). Without loss of generality, we assume the π(v)s sum to one. When π(v) ≡ | V | the problem ( ) becomes the classical finite sum problem in the optimization literature. Instances of non-uniform π arise when training a model with imbalanced data as has been studied in (Steininger et al., ; Wang et al., 2022b; Sow et al., ).",-2,data/raw/icml/2024/first_pages/ICML-2024-P1665.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v235/main/assets/powell24a/powell24a.pdf
