ICML-2024-P1956,ICML-2024-A009657,Bowen Tao,shared,3,3,3,"... 𝛼𝛼 × 𝑾𝑾1 𝒙𝒙 𝜶𝜶𝜽𝜽𝐹𝐹 𝑶𝑶 𝜸𝜸 𝝁𝝁 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 × 𝑾𝑾𝐿𝐿 𝒃𝒃2 × 𝛼𝛼 × 𝑾𝑾2 𝛼𝛼1 𝒃𝒃2 𝛼𝛼2 × 𝑾𝑾2 𝒃𝒃1 × 𝛼𝛼 ... Weight Bias Output 𝜷𝜷 𝝈𝝈 BN ... 𝛼𝛼1 × 𝑾𝑾1 𝒙𝒙 𝒃𝒃1 × 𝛼𝛼1 VSI 𝑶𝑶′ × ∏𝐿𝐿 𝛼𝛼𝑖𝑖 𝑖𝑖= 𝐿𝐿 ... 𝛼𝛼1 × 𝑾𝑾1 𝑶𝑶′ 𝒃𝒃𝐿𝐿 × �𝑖𝑖=1𝛼𝛼𝑖𝑖 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 × 𝑾𝑾𝐿𝐿 𝛼𝛼2 × 𝑾𝑾2 𝒃𝒃2 × 𝛼𝛼2 𝛼𝛼1 𝛼𝛼1 × 𝑾𝑾1 𝒙𝒙 ... 𝒃𝒃1 × 𝛼𝛼1 RSI ... 𝛼𝛼2 × 𝜸𝜸 𝒙𝒙 Surely Scale Invariant: GSI, RSI, NSI 𝛼𝛼 MLI Formula: 𝜶𝜶𝜽𝜽𝑭𝑭 + ( − 𝜶𝜶)𝜽𝜽𝟎𝟎 Plateau 𝒃𝒃𝐿𝐿 × 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 ... 𝜷𝜷 × 𝛼𝛼2 𝛼𝛼1 𝝁𝝁 𝛼𝛼1 𝝈𝝈 𝛼𝛼1 × 𝑾𝑾1 Scale Invariance Nearly Scale Invariant: 𝛼𝛼𝜃𝜃𝐹𝐹 𝒙𝒙 GSI 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 × 𝑾𝑾𝐿𝐿 ... ∏𝐿𝐿𝑖𝑖= 𝛼𝛼𝑖𝑖 𝒃𝒃1 × 𝛼𝛼1 NSI A Special Noise An Inverse Perspective of MLI 𝛼𝛼 Figure . The illustration of the MLI formula. The left shows several types of scale invariance property for various DNN cases. The right explains the MLI formula from a novel perspective, i.e., adding diverse levels of noise perturbation to a nearly scaleinvariant network. The MLI phenomenon emerges from the combined influence of scale invariance and noise robustness. suggesting that DNNs may not be as complex as previously thought. Specifically, Goodfellow & Vinyals ( ) introduces the linear interpolation between the parameters θ at initialization and the parameters θ F at the local minima found after training with SGD. The interpolation, denoted as ( − α)θ + αθ F , demonstrates a monotonic decrease in loss or error as α ranges from to . Goodfellow & Vinyals ( ) attributes MLI to the relative ease of these tasks from an optimization perspective. However, Frankle ( ) observes the MLI for DNNs on more complicated tasks, despite the presence of long plateaus in the loss and error curves. Wang et al. ( ) concludes that the plateau is caused by the bias term and network depth, rather than the difficulty of optimization. These studies excessively focus on investigating the correlation between MLI and the training trajectory, ignoring the formula of MLI itself. . Introduction Deep neural networks (DNNs) are generally considered non-convex models known for their challenging optimization. The stochastic gradient descent (SGD) optimizer is widely utilized in training complex networks (Tao et al., ; Li et al., 2022a;b). Typically, the loss values or test errors exhibit oscillations during training, indicating a rugged loss landscape and a winding optimization trajectory. However, Goodfellow & Vinyals ( ) observes a phenomenon called Monotonic Linear Interpolation (MLI), 𝒃𝒃𝐿𝐿 × 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 Invariant 𝒃𝒃 𝑶𝑶′ × 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 × 𝑾𝑾𝐿𝐿 Scaling Effect 𝑊𝑊 𝛼𝛼𝐿𝐿 𝒃𝒃𝐿𝐿 × 𝛼𝛼 ... 𝛼𝛼 × 𝑾𝑾2 𝑶𝑶′ × Test Error 𝛼𝛼 × 𝑾𝑾𝐿𝐿 Monotonic Linear Interpolation (MLI) refers to the peculiar phenomenon that the error between the initial and converged model monotonically decreases along the linear interpolation, i.e., ( − α)θ + αθ F . Previous works focus on paired initial and converged points, relating MLI to the smoothness of the optimization trajectory. In this paper, we find a shocking fact that the error curves still exhibit a monotonic decrease when θ is replaced with noise or even zero values, implying that the decreasing curve may be primarily related to the property of the converged model rather than the optimization trajectory. We further explore the relationship between αθ F and θ F and propose scale invariance properties in various cases, including Generalized Scale Invariance (GSI), Rectified Scale Invariance (RSI), and Normalized Scale Invariance (NSI). From an inverse perspective, the MLI formula is essentially an equation that adds varying levels of noise (i.e., ( − α)ϵ) to a nearly scale-invariant network (i.e., αθ F ), resulting in a monotonically increasing error as the noise level rises. MLI is a special case where ϵ is equal to θ . 𝑶𝑶 Test Error",-2,data/raw/icml/2024/first_pages/ICML-2024-P1956.txt,https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v235/main/assets/tao24a/tao24a.pdf
